<h2>A large, stable trade lane — not a policy-volatile one</h2>
<p>Bangladesh takes roughly 62% of India's masoor export value and volume — by far the largest single destination for this product, at around USD 92 million in 2024. Unlike the onion trade on the same route, this is a settled, policy-stable lane: masoor has been Free to export since DGFT lifted the general pulses export prohibition in September 2017, and that status has held without the repeated Minimum Export Price and duty interventions that define fresh onion's story. See our <a href="/products/pulses/chickpeas/">chickpeas page</a> for the fuller 2017 policy history, which applies equally to masoor.</p>

<h2>Sea or land — the same choice, for a different product</h2>
<p>As with most goods on this route, sea freight to Chattogram (2–5 days) works for full-container volumes, while the land ports at Petrapole–Benapole (1–3 days) can be faster for smaller or more time-sensitive orders. Masoor's 12+ month shelf life in cool, dry, pest-controlled storage gives you more routing flexibility than a perishable product would — you can weight the decision toward cost and convenience rather than urgency.</p>

<h2>Whole or split — a real specification, not a formality</h2>
<p>Bangladesh imports both whole (husk-on) masoor and split masoor dal, and which one a buyer wants usually reflects their own processing setup — some Bangladeshi importers split and pack domestically, others want dal ready to move into retail or food-service supply chains. Confirm this before quoting, since it changes both the product and, often, the price.</p>

<h2>LC lead time belongs in your schedule</h2>
<p>Bangladesh trade runs overwhelmingly on letters of credit, and Bangladesh Bank's regulatory process for opening and amending LCs adds real time on top of shipping transit. We'd encourage planning your shipment date from LC operative status rather than order confirmation — it's the single most common source of timeline surprise we see on this lane.</p>

